  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:bounded-effective-certificate",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Let \\(c\\in\\mathcal C_K\\cap\\mathbb Q^K\\), \\(\\eta\\in\\mathbb Q_{>0}\\), and \\(L,U\\in\\mathbb Q\\) with \\(L<U\\).  There is a terminating finite augmentation of \\(\\mathsf{Cert}\\) that calls the binary certificate at tolerance \\(\\eta/(U-L)^2\\), stores \\(L,U\\), and returns a total evaluator whose exact output at every integer \\(n\\ge N\\) is\n\\[\n\\bigl((U-L)^2B_n,(U-L)^2U_n\\bigr)\\in\\mathbb A_{\\mathrm R}^2.\n\\]\nThese values satisfy\n\\[\n(U-L)^2B_n\\le R_n^{[L,U]}(c)\\le (U-L)^2U_n,\n\\qquad\nn^{4/3}(U-L)^2(U_n-B_n)\\le\\eta.\n\\]\nThe lower endpoint is the exact Bayes risk of the endpoint-scaled finite-schedule prior, and the upper endpoint is the exact scaled binary risk witness for the Bernoulliized, Rao--Blackwellized total full-label rule under the same allocation \\(q^\\star\\).  The mathematical expansion and coefficient extend to every real \\(c\\in\\mathcal C_K\\) and every finite real \\(L<U\\), but no Turing evaluator is asserted for an unrepresented real contrast or endpoint.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "K",
      "c",
      "\\mathcal C_K",
      "\\eta",
      "L",
      "U",
      "\\mathsf{Cert}",
      "N",
      "n",
      "B_n",
      "U_n",
      "\\mathbb A_{\\mathrm R}",
      "R_n^{[L,U]}(c)",
      "q^\\star"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:contrast-class",
      "def:risk-game",
      "def:effective-certificate",
      "prop:bounded-outcome-reduction",
      "thm:universal-airy-law",
      "thm:effective-certificate",
      "thm:bounded-universal-airy-law"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "Put \\(D=U-L\\in\\mathbb Q_{>0}\\) and run \\(\\mathsf{Cert}(c,\\eta/D^2)\\).  By \\(\\mathrm{thm:effective\\text{-}certificate}\\), this terminates and returns \\(N\\), a finite record, and exact values \\((B_n,U_n)\\in\\mathbb A_{\\mathrm R}^2\\) for every \\(n\\ge N\\), with\n\\[\nB_n\\le R_n^{\\{0,1\\}}(c)\\le U_n,\n\\qquad\nn^{4/3}(U_n-B_n)\\le\\eta/D^2.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nSince \\(D^2\\) is a positive rational number, multiplication by \\(D^2\\) is an exact field operation in \\(\\mathbb A_{\\mathrm R}\\).  The endpoint map \\(w_{i,a}\\mapsto L+Dw_{i,a}\\) sends the binary finite-schedule prior witnessing \\(B_n\\) to a prior supported on \\(\\{L,U\\}^{n\\times K}\\subset[L,U]^{n\\times K}\\).  Equation (6) in the endpoint reduction proves that its target is exactly \\(D\\tau_c(w)\\); posterior conditional means and posterior squared errors therefore scale by \\(D\\) and \\(D^2\\), respectively.  Its exact Bayes risk is consequently \\(D^2B_n\\), which is a valid lower witness for the bounded game.\n\nFor the upper witness, apply formula (1) in the reduction proof to the exact binary rule whose worst-schedule risk is \\(U_n\\).  Inequality (3) proves, for every bounded schedule, that the resulting total observed-data rule under the unchanged design has risk at most \\(D^2U_n\\).  Thus the exact algebraic number \\(D^2U_n\\) is an upper risk witness.  Multiplying (18) by \\(D^2\\) and invoking the exact identity in \\(\\mathrm{prop:bounded\\text{-}outcome\\text{-}reduction}\\) gives\n\\[\nD^2B_n\\le R_n^{[L,U]}(c)\\le D^2U_n,\n\\qquad\nn^{4/3}D^2(U_n-B_n)\\le\\eta.\n\\]\nAppending the rational pair \\((L,U)\\) and the instruction to multiply both evaluator outputs by \\(D^2\\) is a finite operation, and the resulting evaluator is total because the binary evaluator is total.  The real-contrast statement follows either by multiplying the binary expansion from \\(\\mathrm{thm:universal\\text{-}airy\\text{-}law}\\) by \\(D^2\\), or directly from \\(\\mathrm{thm:bounded\\text{-}universal\\text{-}airy\\text{-}law}\\).  The root-risk modulus scales to \\(D\\omega(c,c')\\) by the exact reduction, so the same \\(o(n^{-5/6})\\) rational bracketing transfers the coefficient for each fixed real \\(D>0\\).  This last argument is mathematical and does not encode an unrepresented real input.",
    "justification": "Exact multiplication by \\((U-L)^2\\) extends every binary certificate endpoint and its all-future normalized-width guarantee to rational bounded intervals.",
    "gap": "No cited bounded two-arm result provides exact all-future algebraic witnesses for arbitrary rational fixed contrasts.",
    "consumer": "Trial planners can verify at every horizon whether the bounded-outcome contrast-weighted design is second-order optimal.",
